locationType
  The Maw — a submerged resort that surfaces to feed
  The Signal Tower — a spire that broadcasts nightmares into sleeping minds
  The Nest — a decaying mansion where children are kept like songbirds
  The Pale City — a place of endless rain and televised eyes
  The Schoolhouse — where the lesson is obedience and the pupils never graduate
  The Hospital — a sanatorium where the patients are the furniture
  The Ferris Wheel — a permanent carnival that turns slowly, forever
  The Lighthouse — a beacon that does not guide ships to shore, but away from it
  The Weaver's Mill — where threads are spun from breath and memory
  The Library of Echoes — every book is a child's final thought
  The Bathhouse — where the Guests come to be cleaned of their sins, and their skin
  The Toy Factory — where playthings are made from things that once played
  The Greenhouse — where something is being grown, and it is almost ripe
  The Mausoleum Train — a railway through the dark, with no first station and no last

locationName
  The Drowning Halls
  The Whispering Quarters
  The Cellar of Counted Children
  The Hall of Long Shadows
  The Nursery of Broken Toys
  The Kitchen Where No One Eats
  The Garden of Sleeping Eyes
  The Attic of Forgotten Names
  The Boiler Room of Quiet Screams
  The Ballroom of the Dancing Dead
  The Clocktower of Stopped Time
  The Mirror Maze of Selves
  The Library of Unwritten Books
  The Chapel of the Unspoken Prayer
  The Sewers of Soft Things
  The Rooftop of Longing
  The Pantry of Preserved Children
  The Workshop of the Doll-Maker
  The Morgue of the Unmourned
  The Observatory of Watching Eyes
  The Drying Rooms
  The Hall of Filed Teeth
  The Staircase That Goes Down
  The Sorting Office of Lost Children
  The Conservatory of Broken Music
  The Wards of the Sleepless
  The Mailroom of Unopened Letters
  The Kennels
  The Nave of the Watching Saints
  The Coal Cellar of Slow Combustion
  The Vestibule of Greeting Teeth
  The Laundry of Skins
  The Cabinet of Curiosities
  The Engine Room of Quiet Gears
  The Drawing Room of the Polite Horror

locationDesc
  The halls are lined with portraits whose eyes follow you — not the painted eyes, but the ones behind the canvas. The wallpaper sweats. A dinner bell rings in the distance, and somewhere, something vast pulls out a chair.
  Everything here is too large — the doorknobs at chest height, the stairs built for giants, the spoons you could bathe in. You are an intruder in a dollhouse built for something that eats dolls.
  The floor is warm and gives slightly underfoot. You realize, after too long, that it is breathing. The walls are close and getting closer. The ceiling drips not water, but something thicker.
  It is always dusk here. The light is the color of old honey and lies about everything it touches. Shadows pool in corners and do not match the things that cast them. A music box plays a song you almost remember from a life you almost had.
  The air tastes of copper and perfume. Every surface is sticky. The Residents move through these halls as though underwater, slow and inevitable, and they are always, always heading in your direction.
  Silence here is not the absence of sound — it is a presence, a weight, a thing with texture. When it breaks, you will wish it hadn't. The echo of your own footsteps sounds like a second pair, arriving late.
  This place was beautiful once. You can see it in the moldings, the chandeliers, the sweeping staircase. Whatever ruined it was slow, patient, and thorough. The beauty remains only to make the ruin more cruel.
  The corridors rearrange when you are not looking. You have left breadcrumbs. The breadcrumbs have moved. The door you came through is now a wall. The wall where the door should be is now breathing.
  Everything is damp. The smell is of seaweed and something sweeter underneath — rotting fruit, perhaps, or rotting hope. The tide is coming in. You can hear it. You can hear what the tide brings.

locationDanger
  the Residents, who patrol on a schedule you have not yet learned
  the Hunger, which rises from the floor drains at the turning of the hour
  the View, a vast eye that opens in the ceiling and watches until it finds you
  the Children, who were not saved and do not wish you to be either
  the Architecture itself, which contracts slowly, like a throat swallowing
  the Bell, which when rung summons something from below that cannot be sent back
  the Mirrors, which have begun showing rooms that do not exist — and things in them
  the Tide, which rises without warning and brings up what was submerged
  the Song, which compels all who hear it to walk toward its source, smiling

locationSecret
  Beneath the floorboards is a network of tunnels built by children — a shadow map of escape routes, maintained for a century.
  The chandelier in the main hall is held by a single rusted bolt. Dropping it kills the lights — and something that only hunts in the light.
  A Guest left behind a diary describing a way out, but the last page was torn out by someone who did not want it found.
  There is a room that does not appear on any map. Inside is a single chair, facing a mirror. The mirror shows the way out. The chair is always occupied when you look away.
  The boiler, if fed a specific combination of objects, produces not steam but a door — temporary, leaden, leading somewhere green and lit by a real sun.
  A child's drawing is pinned behind a painting. It shows an exit through the laundry chute, marked with a crude smiley face. The chute is real. So is what lives in it.

