gamePremise
  You wake in a place you do not remember entering. The walls are warm. The door is locked from the outside. Someone has left a single hard candy on a plate by your head, and a note that reads: "Eat this last."
  A festival is being prepared. You are not a guest. You are the main course. You have until the bell rings six times to find the way out — or become part of the celebration.
  Children have been disappearing from a coastal town for generations. You are the first to come back. You did not come back alone. Something followed you out of the dark, and it is hungry for the children you left behind.
  You are the lantern-bearer of a procession that never arrives. The others have fallen, one by one, into the dark between the lights. You are the last. The lantern is growing dim. The dark is growing bold.
  You receive a letter from a sibling you thought was dead. It contains a key and an address. The address is a building that should not exist. The key fits a door inside that opens onto a corridor that goes down for a very long time.
  You are a doll-maker's child, and you have just realized that you are not the child — you are the doll, and you have been dreaming you were alive. The dream is ending. The maker is waking.
  A storm strands you in a guesthouse that has no other guests — only staff, who are very attentive, and very patient, and very interested in how much you weigh.
  You are the last child in a village that has been emptying one family at a time. The adults have all gone to "the Meeting." You do not know what the Meeting is. You are about to find out. You will wish you hadn't.
  You inherit a music box. When wound, it plays a tune that opens doors to places that should not be. One of those places has something in it that has been waiting for the box to be wound again. It has been patient.
  You are small. The world is large. This has always been true. Today, the world has noticed you, and it has decided you are exactly the right size to fit in a very specific hole.
  You are the night porter of a hotel whose guests check in but never check out. Your job is to keep them comfortable. Your other job, the one no one mentions, is to make sure they never leave. Tonight, a child has arrived in a crate marked "STAFF." The child is not staff. The guests are hungry. The manager is watching.
  You fell asleep on a train. You woke on a different train. The other passengers do not acknowledge you. The stations have names you cannot read. The train does not stop. The conductor is coming through. You do not have a ticket.
  You are the newest exhibit in a museum of living children. The curators are kind, in their way. They feed you, they polish the glass. The other exhibits have been here a long time. They do not move much anymore. The tour is tomorrow. You would prefer not to be toured.
  Every night, the lighthouse beam sweeps the town, and whoever it pauses on is taken. You have evaded it for seventeen years. Your younger sibling has not. You are going to the lighthouse. The lighthouse is aware of this.
  You inherit a house from an aunt you never met. The house has seven rooms. Six are locked. The seventh is the one you wake in. The locks on the other six are on the inside. Your aunt left a note: "Do not let them out, whatever they say."
  You are a sleepwalker, and every night you walk somewhere you should not. During the day, you try to retrace your steps. During the night, you go further. Something has been waiting for you to walk all the way to it. You are almost there.
  The orphanage has been empty for years. You are the last child, left behind when the others were adopted. You were not forgotten. You were reserved. The one who reserved you is coming to collect. You have until the bell rings to find the one door they did not lock.
  You are a glassblower's apprentice in a city where the glass is made from breath. Every breath taken from a child makes a pane. The great window of the cathedral is almost complete. It needs one more breath. Yours. You have learned what the window shows when the light comes through. You will not be the pane.
  The carnival has come to town. It never leaves. The rides turn slowly, forever. The Barker knows every child by name. You went in once, years ago, and came out. The Barker remembers. The carnival has followed you home. It is set up in your street. It is waiting for you to come back in.
  You are a census-taker, sent to count the inhabitants of a village that appears on no map. The villagers are very willing to be counted. They are very interested in being counted. They keep asking when you will be counted. You realize, slowly, that the census is a menu.

