chapterDesc
  You arrive. The place greets you with the silence of a held breath. Something here has been expecting you. It is patient. It has been patient for a long time.
  The Residents go about their routines, blind to your presence — for now. You learn their patterns: when they eat, when they sleep, when they turn their vast backs. You learn to move in the gaps between their attention.
  You find other children. They do not help you. They have been here too long. They watch you with the flat eyes of fish in a tank, and one of them mouths a single word, over and over, that you cannot quite read.
  The first true horror. A Resident sees you. The chase begins — through halls too large, over furniture built for giants, under gaps that barely fit your body. You escape. You do not forget the sound of their breathing.
  You discover the truth of this place — or a piece of it. A diary, a painting, a room that should not exist. The truth is worse than the fear. The fear was keeping you moving. The truth makes you want to stop.
  A moment of peace, fragile as a held note. A warm room, a found blanket, a morsel of food that is not what it seems but you eat it anyway because you are so, so hungry. Something here is almost kind. Almost.
  The trap closes. The exit you found is sealed. The Residents have changed their patterns — they are hunting now, not patrolling. The place itself seems to lean against you, herding you somewhere you do not wish to go.
  You meet the one who rules this place. They do not chase you. They do not need to. They speak, and their voice is the voice of every adult who ever told you to be good, to be quiet, to be small. You feel yourself wanting to obey.
  The final ascent. Everything you have learned — every pattern, every hiding spot, every weakness — comes together. You climb. The place falls away beneath you. Something vast follows, no longer patient, no longer slow.
  The ending. The door. The choice. Whatever waits on the other side, you will not be the same child who crawled in through the cellar grate. The place has marked you. The question is whether the mark is a scar or a hunger.
  A long descent through a service stair, past murals that depict children at play — the children in the murals are not the same ones each time you look. One of them is you now.
  You find the kitchens after hours. The pots are still warm. The ladle is still wet. The cook is not here. The cook has never been here, you realize, and yet the meals are always prepared. You are being fed. You wonder by whom.
  A ceremony is in progress. You watch from above, through a grate. The participants wear robes and chant in a language you almost know. At the center of the circle is a small chair. The chair is empty. It is waiting.
  You are sorted. You do not know into what. You stand in a line of children. One by one, they are taken through a door. Some come back changed. Some do not come back. The line moves forward. You are closer to the door.
  You find the cages. Most are empty. Some are not. The things in the occupied cages look at you with eyes you recognize. One of them says your name. You do not open the cage. You are not sure you are right not to.
  A room of unanswered letters, thousands of them, addressed to children who never received them. You find one with your name on it. It is from someone you do not remember. It tells you where the door is. It also tells you what waits on the other side.
  You enter a vast counting house where a Resident tallies things on an abacus of bones. The tally includes a column labeled with your name. The number beside it is going up. You do not know what is being counted. You suspect it is days.
  You descend into a well that is also a stair, that is also a throat. The walls are damp and warm and close. At the bottom is a pool of black water that reflects a sky that is not the one above. Something in the water is looking up at you.
  A party is being held in your honor. You did not RSVP. The guests are effusive. The food is extraordinary. You cannot leave until the host has spoken to you. The host is late. The host is always late. The guests are growing impatient, and hungry, and honest.
  The final room. It is small. It is warm. It is exactly the room you have always wanted. There is a bed, and a meal, and a door that you may open or not. There is someone already in the bed. They look like you. They are sleeping. You may join them, or you may leave. There is no third option.

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ENDINGS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
endingType
  The Escape — you find the door, and this time it opens. You do not look back. You never look back. But sometimes, in mirrors, you see the hallway behind you, and it is closer than it was yesterday.
  The Sacrifice — you cannot both escape. You stay behind so the smaller one can go. The door closes. You turn to face the place that is now your home. You are no longer the child. You are beginning to be the Resident.
  The Hunger — you escape, but something came with you. You try to eat and the food has no taste. You try to sleep and the dreams are of corridors. You are beginning to understand why the Guests never truly leave.
  The Transformation — you do not escape. You grow. The place feeds you, in its way, and you grow until the doorknobs are at your height and the stairs fit your feet. You do not remember being small. You are a Resident now.
  The Inheritance — you kill the one who rules, or they simply hand you the key. The place is yours now. You feel its weight settle onto your shoulders like a coat. The other children look up at you with hope. You feel the first stirring of hunger.
  The Loop — the door opens onto the cellar where you began. You are smaller now. You do not remember being here before. A plate with a single hard candy sits by where your head will lie. A note reads: "Eat this last."
  The Light — you find the sun. Actual sun. It burns, after so long in the dark. You stand in it until it stops burning, and then you walk away from the place and you do not stop walking. You are free. You are also very, very hungry, and the world is full of small, soft things.
  The Becoming — you do not leave. You do not stay. You become the place. The walls are your skin, the halls your throat, the Residents your fingers. The next child who crawls in through the cellar grate will find you waiting, patient, vast, and so very hungry.
  The Reunion — you do not escape alone. You find them. The one you came for. They are not what they were, but they are them, and you take them out into a world that does not know what to do with either of you. You are together. That will have to be enough.
  The Bargain — you do not escape. You negotiate. You offer the place something it wants more than you, and it lets you walk. You do not look at what you offered. You hear it, sometimes, in the dark, asking if you are coming back. You are not. You are not sure.
  The Inheritance Refused — you are offered the place. The key. The hunger. You say no. This has never happened before. The place does not know what to do. It lets you go, confused, almost offended. You leave it standing in its own hallway, holding a key it cannot give away.
  The Sleep — you do not escape, but you are not caught. You find a small room, and a warm bed, and you lie down. You sleep. The place does not disturb you. You sleep for a very long time. When you wake, the place is empty. Everyone is gone. You are the only one left. You are not sure you are awake.
  The Door That Was Not There — you find a door that was not there yesterday. It opens onto a place you do not recognize, that is not the place you came from, and not the place you are. You step through. The door closes. You are somewhere. It is quiet. You are almost sure it is better.
  The Witness — you do not escape. You cannot. But you survive, and you remember, and you scratch the names of every child you knew into the walls, so that the next one who comes will know they were not alone, that there were others, that they fought. You are the walls' memory now. The next child will read your name. They will go on.
  The Child Who Grew Down — you do not grow up. You grow down. Smaller, smaller, until you fit in the cracks, in the pipes, in the gaps between floors. You become a stowaway in your own story. You live in the walls. You watch the next child arrive. You try to warn them. You have forgotten how to speak. You are still trying.
  The Mirror — you escape through a mirror. On the other side is your old life, unchanged, as if you never left. You are grateful. You are suspicious. You check the mirrors in your new home. They all show the place you left. In one of them, something moves. It is wearing your face. It is patient. It is learning.

endingTone
  bittersweet — freedom tastes of ash and old rain
  hopeful — against all odds, a small light persists
  bleak — the place wins. It always wins. But the child fought, and that matters, somehow.
  ambiguous — you are out. Or you are dreaming you are out. The dream feels thin at the edges.
  triumphant — you brought the place down around its own ears. You walked out through the rubble. You are very small and the sky is very large and you are crying and laughing and you are free.
  quietly devastating — you sit on the shore and watch the place sink. The water is still. You are alone. You have always been alone. But now you are alone in the open air, and that is something.
  grimly triumphant — you won, but winning looks like survival, and survival looks like a child sitting alone in a room, breathing
  melancholic — the door opened. you walked through. the world did not celebrate. the world did not notice. you are free, and freedom is very quiet
  ominous — you are out. you are safe. you are also certain, in a way you cannot explain, that the place let you go. places do not let children go. you are waiting to find out why
  tender — you did not escape the place. the place escaped the world. you sit with the last of it, a small fading thing, and you hold its hand as it goes out. you are the only one who mourned it
  unresolved — the door is open. the light is real. you do not go through. you do not go back. you sit on the threshold and you cannot remember which side is home
  heavy — you made it. you carry everyone who did not. you will carry them for the rest of your life. the weight is the only proof they existed. you would not put it down even if you could

